==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*keh₂p-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|recupero|recuperāre}}, a late form of {{m|la|recipero|reciperāre|t=get again, regain, recover}}. {{doublet|en|recover|recoup}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈk(j)uːpəˌɹeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-recuperate.wav |Audio (Southern England)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[recover]], especially from an [[illness]]; to get better from an illness or from exhaustion (or sometimes from a financial loss, etc).
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[restore]] (someone or something) to health, [[strength]], or [[currency]]; to [[revive]] or [[rehabilitate]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1901|author=Edward Harper Parker|title=China, Her History, Diplomacy, and Commerce: From the Earliest Times to the Present Day|publisher=London : Murray|page=191
|text=[...] of each province in 1842 and 1894 - that is, before the Taiping rebellion, and since China has '''recuperated''' her forces.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2015-03-09|author=Gary Day|author2=Jack Lynch|title=The Encyclopedia of British Literature, 3 Volume Set: 1660 - 1789|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|isbn=9781444330205|page=494
|text=[...] one of many female poets who was trivialized and misrepresented for decades. When William Wordsworth '''recuperated''' her by praising her “Nocturnal Reverie,” he set what became a limiting factor in Finch's recovery: he treated her as a pre-Romantic ppoet of nature, and she became resituated in literary history as a much flatter or less complicated poet than she was in her lifetime.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[recover]]; to [[regain]].
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2015-08-01|author=Cristina Herrera|author2=Paula Sanmartín|title=Reading/Speaking/Writing the Mother Text; Essays on Caribbean Women's Writing|publisher=Demeter Press|isbn=9781772580273
|text=In LS, July emerges as a survivor and a storyteller with a traumatic past who has '''recuperated''' her relationship with her lost son. Her questioning and humorously subversive discourse gives emotional and textual depth to {{nb...}}}}
#* {{seeMoreCites|en}}
# {{rfv-sense|en}} {{lb|en|sociology}} To co-opt subversive ideas for mainstream use.
#* {{quote-book|en|2002|Roger Beebe, Denise Fulbrook, Ben Saunders|Rock Over the Edge: Transformations in Popular Music Culture|passage={{...}} there is also the danger {{...}} that such a critique '''recuperates''' gender in terms that quite literally invisiblize the very issues of race and ethnicity {{...}}}}

